\emph{Step 5: why the phase does not interfere.} If $\varphi$ is fitted too, linearize in both parameters: the model changes by $g_n\,\delta\omega+h_n\,\delta\varphi$ with $h_n=A\cos(\omega_0u_n+\varphi)$. Least squares for $(\delta\omega,\delta\varphi)$ then gives the $2\times2$ normal equations
\begin{equation}
\begin{pmatrix}\sum_ng_n^2&\sum_ng_nh_n\\ \sum_ng_nh_n&\sum_nh_n^2\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\delta\omega\\ \delta\varphi\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\sum_ng_nd_n\\ \sum_nh_nd_n\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
The off-diagonal entry is $\sum_ng_nh_n=A^2\sum_nu_n\cos^2(\omega_0u_n+\varphi)=\tfrac{A^2}{2}\sum_nu_n+\tfrac{A^2}{2}\sum_nu_n\cos\bigl(2(\omega_0u_n+\varphi)\bigr)$. The first sum is close to zero because $u$ is measured from the window centre, so positive and negative $u_n$ cancel; the second oscillates and is small compared with the diagonal entries. Neglecting the off-diagonal entry, the matrix is diagonal, and its first row reads $\bigl(\sum_ng_n^2\bigr)\delta\omega=\sum_ng_nd_n$, exactly the one-parameter equation of Step 2. The variance of Step 3 is therefore unchanged. The two parameters decouple, which is why we centred the time variable.
